\section{System Model}
\label{sec:sys_model}

We consider an SCPN built upon a classic Walker Delta LEO constellation, where satellites are connected through laser ISLs and equipped with constrained onboard computing and storage resources.
Microservice applications are deployed as stateless replicas across satellites, allowing each request to be collaboratively executed through successive communication and computation stages.

% We consider a space computing power network (SCPN) composed of a Walker-Delta LEO constellation, where each satellite is equipped with onboard computing resources and laser inter-satellite links (ISLs).
% User tasks are represented as ordered microservice chains.
% Each microservice can have multiple stateless replicas deployed on different satellites, and a request may therefore be executed collaboratively by multiple satellites.
% The key distinction from terrestrial edge systems is that the SCPN simultaneously exhibits predictable but rapid topology dynamics, intermittent inter-plane ISLs, limited onboard compute and storage resources, and time-varying communication conditions.

\subsection{LEO Constellation and Topology Model}
\label{subsec:constellation_model_new}

A typical Walker Delta constellation is parameterized by $\mathcal{W}=(N_{\text{sat}}/P/G,h_{\text{o}},I)$, where $N_{\text{sat}}$, $P$, $G$, $h_{\text{o}}$, and $I$ denote the number of satellites, orbital planes, phasing factor, altitude, and inclination, respectively. 
Consequently, each orbital plane contains $N=N_{\text{sat}}/P$ satellites. We define $\mathcal{V}=\{v_{p,n}\mid p=1,\ldots,P,\ n=1,\ldots,N\}$ as the set of satellites in the constellation.
To capture the dynamism of the satellite network, we divide the constellation period into $T$ slots of duration $\Delta$. For any continuous time $\tau$, its corresponding slot index is $s(\tau)=\lfloor\tau/\Delta\rfloor\bmod T$. 
\begin{comment}
Following a snapshot-based modeling approach~\cite{hier_learn_zhu2025}, we assume that the network state, including satellite positions, ISL availability, and capacity, remains unchanged within a slot and evolves only at slot boundaries.
We utilize a time-dependent graph $\mathcal{G}(t) = \big(\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}(t)\big)$ to model the time-varying network topology, where $\mathcal{E}(t)$ represents the set of feasible ISLs at slot $t$.
\end{comment}
Following a snapshot-based modeling approach~\cite{hier_learn_zhu2025}, we assume that the network state, including satellite positions, ISL availability, and capacity, remains unchanged within a slot and evolves only at slot boundaries.
We use the graph $\mathcal{G}(t) = \big(\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}(t)\big)$ to model the network topology in slot $t$, where $\mathcal{E}(t)$ represents the set of feasible ISLs.

\subsection{ISL Communication Model}
We assume that each satellite is equipped with four laser optical terminals: two positioned along the roll axis for intra-plane ISLs, and two along the pitch axis for inter-plane ISLs.


\paragraph{ISL Connectivity Constraints}
Each satellite $v_{p,n}$ maintains two intra-plane ISLs with its two adjacent neighbors $v_{p,n^-}$ and $v_{p,n^+}$.
Because satellites in the same plane share identical angular velocities, their relative distances remain time-invariant, yielding highly stable communication links.

Conversely, inter-plane ISLs are dynamic.
Constrained by the physical limits of onboard laser communication terminals, the existence of such a link $e=(u,v)$ in slot $t$ is jointly determined by the line of sight (LoS) distance, mechanical tracking thresholds, and polar exclusions, formulated as:
\begin{equation}
e\in \mathcal{E}(t) \iff \left\{
\begin{array}{l}
d_{u,v}(t) \le d_{\text{max}}, \\
\theta_{u,v}(t) \le \theta_{\text{max}}, \\
\max\big(|\varphi_u(t)|, |\varphi_v(t)|\big) \le \varphi_{\text{max}},
\end{array}
\right.
\label{eq:cross_link_indicator}
\end{equation}
where the LoS distance $d_{u,v}(t)$ and relative tracking angle $\theta_{u,v}(t)$ are bounded by the terrestrial occlusion limit $d_{\text{max}}$ and the hardware threshold $\theta_{\text{max}}$, respectively.
Furthermore, the third constraint enforces a polar exclusion zone at latitude boundary $\varphi_{\text{max}}$ (e.g., $70^{\circ}$), by proactively tearing down inter-plane links, thereby avoiding the severe pointing dynamics and extreme Doppler shifts inherent to polar regions.
% This mechanism proactively tears down inter-plane links at high latitudes to bypass the severe pointing dynamics and extreme Doppler shifts inherent to polar regions.


\paragraph{ISL Capacity Model}
% Under the Free-Space Optical (FSO) communication model, the received optical power $P_{e}^{\text{rx}}(t)$ at satellite $v$ transmitted from satellite $u$ is formalized by the Friis transmission equation variant:
%Unlike terrestrial networks with stable capacities, the transmission rate of laser ISLs is distance-dependent due to geometric propagation loss.
\begin{comment}
Based on the Free-Space Optical (FSO) communication model, the received power $P_{e}^{\text{rx}}(t)$ at slot $t$ transmitted from satellite $u$ to $v$ is formalized as:
\end{comment}
Under the free space optical (FSO) communication model, the power $P_{e}^{\text{rx}}(t)$ received by satellite $v$ from satellite $u$ in slot $t$ is formulated as:
\begin{equation}
P_{e}^{\text{rx}}(t) = P^{\text{tx}} G^{\text{tx}} G^{\text{rx}} \eta_{\text{tx}} \eta_{\text{rx}} \left(\frac{\lambda}{4\pi d_{u,v}(t)}\right)^2,
\label{eq:received_power}
\end{equation}
where $P^{\text{tx}}$ is the laser transmit power, 
%$G^{\text{tx}}$ and $G^{\text{rx}}$ represent the optical gains of the transmitter and receiver apertures, respectively, 
$G^{\text{tx}}$ and $G^{\text{rx}}$ are the optical gains, 
$\lambda$ is the laser wavelength, and $\eta_{\text{tx}}, \eta_{\text{rx}}$ denote the optical efficiency factors.
\begin{comment}
The real-time Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) of the link $e$ at time slot $t$ is given as:
\end{comment}
The signal to noise ratio (SNR) of link $e$ in slot $t$ is calculated by:
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{e}(t) = \frac{\left({R}_{\text{pd}} \cdot P_{e}^{\text{rx}}(t)\right)^2}{\sigma_{\text{sh}}^2 + \sigma_{\text{th}}^2},
\label{eq:snr_formal}
\end{equation}
where ${R}_{\text{pd}}$ is the photodiode responsivity, while $\sigma_{\text{sh}}^2$ and $\sigma_{\text{th}}^2$ denote the shot and thermal noise variances, respectively.
According to the Shannon-Hartley theorem~\cite{IPIS_sate}, the achievable transmission capacity $R^0_{e}(t)$ is constrained by:
\begin{equation}
R^0_{e}(t) =  \mathrm{B}_{e} \log_2 \left( 1 + \gamma_{e}(t) \right),
\label{eq:shannon_capacity}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{B}_{e}$ is the modulation bandwidth.

\paragraph{ISL Delay Model}
The ideal rate $R^0_e(t)$ in \eqref{eq:shannon_capacity} could be degraded in practice by transient channel fading and traffic contention.
To capture these factors, we introduce a scaling factor $\eta_e(t)\in(0,1]$ to formulate the effective data rate as $R_e(t)=\eta_e(t)R^0_e(t)$.
\begin{comment}
When transmitting a data block of size $B$ over link $e=(u,v)$ during slot $t$, the aggregate link-level communication delay comprises queuing, transmission, and propagation delays, formulated as:
\end{comment}
When a data block of size $B$ is transmitted over link $e=(u,v)$ during slot $t$, its communication delay comprises queuing, transmission, and propagation delays:
\begin{equation}
T_e^{\text{cm}}(B,t) = \nu_e^{\text{qe}}(t) + \nu_e^{\text{tr}}(t) + \nu_e^{\text{pr}}(t),
\label{eq:link_delay_elara}
\end{equation}
where $\nu_e^{\text{qe}}(t)$ is the queuing delay, $\nu_e^{\text{tr}}(t) = B / R_e(t)$ is the transmission delay, and $\nu_e^{\text{pr}}(t)=d_e(t)/c$ is the propagation delay, with $c$ denoting the speed of light in vacuum.

%Here, $\nu_e^{\text{qe}}(t)$ denotes the queuing delay, which can be dynamically estimated via lightweight queue-length probing. The transmission delay is given by $\nu_e^{\text{tr}}(t) = B / R_e(t)$, and the propagation delay is c, with $c$ being the speed of light in vacuum.

% Crucially, this abstraction enables ELARA to implicitly account for network congestion without incurring the prohibitive overhead of tracking individual background flows.

% This compact model allows ELARA to account for link contention without explicitly modeling every concurrent background flow.

\subsection{Onboard Computing Model}
\label{subsec:compute_model_elara}
%Let $F^0_v$ denote the ideal computing capacity of satellite $v$.
%In practice, the available computational resources are often constrained by background payload tasks, operating system overhead, and thermal throttling~\cite{joint_orbit_comp_comm4data_Download}.
%To capture these hardware and software limitations, we introduce a computational efficiency factor $\xi_v(t)\in(0,1]$ and define the effective dynamic computing capacity as $F_v(t) = \xi_v(t)F^0_v$.

\begin{comment}
Let $F^0_v$  denote the rated computing capacity of satellite $v$. Due to onboard hardware and software overheads~\cite{joint_orbit_comp_comm4data_Download}, its effective computing capacity may be discounted by an efficiency factor $\xi_v(t)\in(0,1]$, yeilding $F_v(t) = \xi_v(t)F^0_v$.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{Let $F^0_v$ denote the rated computing capacity of satellite $v$. Due to onboard hardware and software overheads~\cite{joint_orbit_comp_comm4data_Download}, its effective computing capacity may be discounted by an efficiency factor $\xi_v(t)\in(0,1]$, yielding $F_v(t) = \xi_v(t)F^0_v$.}
%Let $\nu^{\text{qe}}_v(t)$ denote the queuing delay before microservice $m$ is scheduled for execution on satellite $v$, which increases with the pending task queue length at slot $t$.
\begin{comment}
Let $\nu^{\text{qe}}_v(t)$ denote the queuing delay before service $m$ is scheduled for execution on satellite $v$, the computation delay is given by:
\end{comment}
Let $\nu^{\text{qe}}_v(t)$ denote the queuing delay before serving $m$ on satellite $v$. The formulation of computation delay is denoted by:
\begin{equation}
T_m^{\text{cp}}(v,t) = \nu^{\text{qe}}_v(t) + \nu^{\text{cp}}_v(t),
\label{eq:compute_delay_elara}
\end{equation}
where $\nu^{\text{cp}}_v(t) = C_m / {F_v(t)}$ is the execution delay, and $C_m$ is the number of CPU cycles required by microservice $m$.
% Here, the queuing delay $\tau^{\text{qe}}_v(t)$ is a monotonically increasing function of the pending task queue length on satellite $v$ at slot $t$.
% The actual execution delay is given by $\tau^{\text{cp}}_v(t) = C_m / {F_v(t)}$, where $C_m$ denotes the number of CPU cycles required by microservice $m$.
The corresponding computation energy is:
\begin{align}
E_m^{\text{cp}}(v,t) = P_v^{\text{cp}} \frac{C_m}{F_v(t)},
\label{eq:compute_energy_elara}
\end{align}
where $P_v^{\text{cp}}$ is the operational computing power of the satellite.



\subsection{LEO Microservice and Service Request Models}
\label{subsec:microservice_request_elara}
%To facilitate flexible application management within the SCPN, complex applications are decoupled into a set of fine-grained, loosely coupled microservices, denoted by $\mathcal{M}=\{1, 2, \dots, M \}$.
%Each microservice $m \in \mathcal{M}$ is characterized by its computational demand $C_m$ (in CPU cycles), container image size $I_m$, and storage requirement $S_m$.

Let $\mathcal{M}= \{ 1, 2, \ldots, M \}$ be the microservice set. Each $m \in \mathcal{M}$ is characterized by its computational demand $C_m$, measured in CPU cycles, and its runtime memory requirement $H_m$. We assume that every satellite stores the complete catalog of service images in persistent storage, but only active replicas consume runtime memory.
%Let $x_{m,v}(t)=1$ when $m$ is active on $v$ at $t$, and $\mathcal R_m(t)=\{v\in\mathcal V\mid x_{m,v}(t)=1\}$. The deployment satisfies
Let $x_{m,v}(t)=1$ when $m$ is active on $v$ at $t$. The active services on each satellite $v$ satisfy:
\begin{align}
{\sum}_{m \in \mathcal{ M }}x_{m,v}(t)H_m&\leq H_v,  \qquad \forall v\in\mathcal V,
\label{eq:memory_elara}
\end{align}
%where $H_v$ is the memory capacity of  satellite $v$. Additionally, to ensure service availability while preventing excessive resource contention, the number of active replicas of service $m$ is bounded by $N_{\text{min}}$ and $N_{\text{max}}$, which is given as:
where $H_v$ is the memory capacity of satellite $v$. To ensure service availability while preventing excessive resource contention, the number of active replicas of service $m$ satisfy:
\begin{align}
N_{\text{min}} \leq {\sum}_{v \in \mathcal{V}} x_{m,v}(t)  \leq  N_{\text{max}},  \qquad  \forall m \in \mathcal {M}.
\label{eq:replica_count_elara}
\end{align}
\begin{comment}
At slot $t$, the set of satellites hosting replicas of service $m$ is denoted as: $\mathcal{V}_{m}(t)= \{v \in \mathcal{V}  \mid  x_{m,v}(t) = 1\}$.
\end{comment}
In slot $t$, the set of satellites hosting replicas of service $m$ is denoted by $\mathcal{V}_{m}(t)= \{v \in \mathcal{V}  \mid  x_{m,v}(t) = 1\}$.
%We assume that all microservices are stateless~\cite{komet_socc2024}; therefore, under the pre-stored image catalog, replica relocation requires neither state synchronization nor image transfer over ISLs.

Let $r=\langle v_s,m_1,\ldots,m_L,v_d,\tau_{\text{in}}\rangle$ denote an incoming request, where $v_s$, $v_d$, and $\tau_{\text{in}}$ are the ingress satellite, egress satellite, and arrival time, respectively.
Let $B_{r,i}$ denote the  data volume before invoking service $m_i$, and $B_{r,L+1}$ be the final output.
In this paper, we focus on the process after the request has arrived at the satellite network.
%Before invoking stage $m_i$, $B_{r,i}$ bits must reach a selected replica, $B_{r,L+1}$ is the final output delivered to $v_d$.

%Let $r = \langle v_s, m_1, m_2, \ldots, m_L, v_d, \tau_{\text{in}} \rangle$ denote an incoming request with a deadline $Z_r$.
%Here, $v_s$ and $v_d$ are the source and destination satellites, $m_i$ is the $i$-th microservice in the sequential execution chain, and $\tau_{\text{in}}$ is the continuous arrival time.
%Let $B_{r,i}$ be the data volume that must be transmitted before invoking $m_i$, while $B_{r,L+1}$ denotes the final output routed to $v_d$.

\paragraph{Routing Model across Time Slots}
ELARA tracks the service execution in continuous time and divides the complete process into $L+1$ stages. 
\begin{comment}
For the $i$-th stage, let $u_{r,i}$ denote the satellite holding input data of size $B_{r,i}$, and let $v_{r,i}\in\mathcal{R}_{m_i}\big(s(\tau_{r,i})\big)$ be the selected satellite for executing $m_i$.
\end{comment}
For stage $i$, let $u_{r,i}$ denote the satellite holding input data of size $B_{r,i}$, and let $v_{r,i}\in\mathcal{V}_{m_i}\big(s(\tau_{r,i})\big)$ be the selected satellite for executing $m_i$.
Since requests or data transmissions may start at arbitrary times, for stage $i$ of $r$, a single routing operation can span $Z_{r,i} > 1$ phases (slots).
\begin{comment}
To simplify notation, we omit the indices $(r,i)$ from phase-level variables.
For the $z$-th phase, let $\tau^z$, $t_z=s(\tau^z)$, $\bar{\Delta}_z$, and $B^z$ denote the decision time, active slot, remaining slot duration, and residual data, respectively. Initially, $\tau^1=\tau_{r,i}$ and $B^1=B_{r,i}$. Let $\mathcal{P}^z$ be the set of chosen routing paths from $u_{r,i}$ to $v_{r,i}$, and let $d_p^z$ be the data volume assigned to path $p$.
\end{comment}
To simplify notation, we omit the indices $(r,i)$ from the variables associated with each phase.
For phase $z$, let $\tau^z$, $t_z=s(\tau^z)$, $\bar{\Delta}_z$, and $B^z$ denote the decision time, active slot, remaining slot duration, and residual data, respectively. Initially, $\tau^1=\tau_{r,i}$ and $B^1=B_{r,i}$. Let $\mathcal{P}^z$ be the set of chosen routing paths from $u_{r,i}$ to $v_{r,i}$, and let $d_p^z$ be the data volume assigned to path $p$.
The delivered data over $\mathcal{P}^z$ is $ D^z= \sum_{p\in\mathcal{P}^z} d_p^z \leq B^z $, and the aggregated data load of any link $e \in p$ is bounded by:
\begin{align}
f_e^z = {\sum}_{p \in \mathcal{P}^z : e \in p}d_p^z
\leq R_e(t_z)\bar{\Delta}_z,
\qquad e\in\mathcal{E}(t_z).
\label{eq:link_load_model}
\end{align}
\begin{comment}
$R_e(t_z)$ is the effective transmission rate of link $e$ at slot $t_z$.
So the completion time of path $p$ is $\delta_p^z ={\sum}_{e\in p}T_e^{\text{cm}}\big(f_e^z,t_z\big)$,
and we have the duration of phase $z$ is formulated as:
\end{comment}
Here, $R_e(t_z)$ is the effective transmission rate of link $e$ in slot $t_z$. The completion time of path $p$ is $\delta_p^z ={\sum}_{e\in p}T_e^{\text{cm}}\big(f_e^z,t_z\big)$, and the duration of phase $z$ is:
\begin{align}
\Delta_z^{\text{ru}} = {\max}_{p \in \mathcal{P}^{z}}\delta_p^z,\quad \text{and},\quad
\Delta_z^{\text{ru}}\leq \bar{\Delta}_z.
\label{eq:parallel_phase_delay_model}
\end{align}
Thus, every admitted subflow reaches $v_{r,i}$ within the slot $t_z$. The transmission energy is $ E_z^{\text{ru}} = \sum_{e\in\mathcal{E}(t_z)} P_e^{\text{tx}}{f_e^z}/{R_e(t_z)}$.
\begin{comment}
After being transferred to phase $z+1$, the residual data remains at $u_{r,i}$ is updated as $B^{z+1}=B^z-D^z$.
If $B^{z+1}>0$, the next transmission occurs at the following slot, and updates with ${\tau}^{z+1}={\tau}^{z}+ \bar{\Delta}_z$. If the transfer completes, ${\tau}^{z+1}={\tau}^{z}+ {\Delta}^{\text{ru}}_z$.
In this way, no data is carried across a topology update by an relay satellite.
\end{comment}
After phase $z$, the undelivered data remains at $u_{r,i}$ and is updated as $B^{z+1}=B^z-D^z$.
If $B^{z+1}>0$, the next transmission begins at the following slot boundary, so ${\tau}^{z+1}={\tau}^{z}+ \bar{\Delta}_z$. If the transfer completes, ${\tau}^{z+1}={\tau}^{z}+ {\Delta}^{\text{ru}}_z$.
Thus, no data is carried across a topology update by a relay satellite.
% ELARA waits until the next slot boundary and recomputes the path set $\mathcal{P}^{z+1}$; otherwise, the transfer terminates after $\Delta_{z+1}^{\text{ru}}$.
For stage $i$ of request $r$, the formulars of routing latency and energy are:
\begin{equation}
T_{r,i}^{\text{ru}}={\sum}_{z=1}^{Z_{r,i}}\Delta_z^{\text{ru}},\qquad
E_{r,i}^{\text{ru}}={\sum}_{z=1}^{Z_{r,i}}E_{r,i}^{z,\text{ru}}.
\label{eq:cross_slot_routing_cost_model}
\end{equation}
The input reaches $v_{r,i}$ at
$\tau_{r,i}^{\text{arr}}=\tau_{r,i}+T_{r,i}^{\text{ru}}$, after which $m_i$ is executed according to \eqref{eq:compute_delay_elara} and \eqref{eq:compute_energy_elara}.
\begin{comment}
The cumulative end-to-end latency and total execution energy of request $r$ are:
\end{comment}
The total latency and execution energy of request $r$ are calculated by:
\begin{align}
T_r^{\text{e2e}}
&=
{\sum}_{i=1}^{L+1}T_{r,i}^{\text{ru}}
+
{\sum}_{i=1}^{L}
T_{m_i}^{\text{cp}}
\left(v_{r,i},s\left(\tau_{r,i}^{\text{arr}}\right)\right),
\label{eq:req_e2e_lat}\\
E_r^{\text{tot}}
&=
{\sum}_{i=1}^{L+1}E_{r,i}^{\text{ru}}
+
{\sum}_{i=1}^{L}
E_{m_i}^{\text{cp}}
\left(v_{r,i},s\left(\tau_{r,i}^{\text{arr}}\right)\right),
\label{eq:req_tot_eng}
\end{align}
where $i=L+1$ denotes the delivery of the final output to $v_d$.


\paragraph{Service Replica Adaptation Model}
Let $\mathbf X_w=[x_{m,v}]$ be the deployment in window $w$. At a window boundary, ELARA selects at most $K_{\mathrm{ m}}$ services and assigns each a retention action or a relocation that replaces one active replica. For the selected action set $\mathcal A_w$,
\begin{align}
\mathbf{X}_{w+1} = \Phi(\mathbf{X}_w, \mathcal{A}_w),
\label{eq:deployment_transition}
\end{align}
where $\Phi(\cdot)$ applies actions sequentially while preserving \eqref{eq:replica_count_elara} and \eqref{eq:memory_elara}. A retention action leaves $\mathbf X_w$ unchanged, whereas relocation only adjusts the resident service replica set.

\section{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:prob_form}
% For each incoming request, the controller jointly selects the serving satellite for each microservice, routes intermediate data over time-varying multi-hop ISLs, and periodically adapts the replica deployment.
%The objective of ELARA is to minimize the end-to-end latency and execution energy of service chains, including both communication and computation costs, while accounting for replica redeployment overhead.
The objective of ELARA is to minimize the end-to-end latency and execution energy of service chains subject to communication, computation, and deployment constraints.

\subsection{Decision Variables}
Once microservice $m_{i-1}$ is completed, ELARA selects the serving satellite for $m_i$. Let $y_{r,i,v} \in \{0,1\}$ be the corresponding decision variable, which satisfies:
\begin{equation}
{\sum}_{v\in\mathcal{V}} y_{r,i,v}=1,\quad  y_{r,i,v}\leq x_{m_i,v}\big(s(\tau_{r,i})\big),\quad \forall r,i,v.
\label{eq:serving_unique_elara}
\end{equation}
Given the selected satellite, ELARA constructs the routing policy across slots for data transmission, denoted by:
\begin{align}
\pi^{\text{ru}}_{r,i} = \{D^z_{r,i}, \mathbf{f}^z_{r,i}, \mathcal{P}^z_{r,i}\}_{z=1}^{Z_{r,i}},
\label{eq:service_routing_policy}
\end{align}
\begin{comment}
Additionally, ELARA periodically updates the deployment matrix $\mathbf{X}_{w}$ by applying $\mathcal{A}_{w}$.
Thus, $\pi^{\text{se}}$, $\pi^{\text{ru}}$, and $\pi^{\text{de}}$ determine the $\{y_{r,i,v}\}$, $\{D^z_{r,i}, \mathbf{f}^z_{r,i}, \mathcal{P}^z_{r,i}\}_{z=1}^{Z_{r,i}}$, and $\{\mathcal A_w\}$, respectively.
\end{comment}
ELARA also updates the deployment matrix $\mathbf{X}_{w}$ periodically by applying $\mathcal{A}_{w}$.
Thus, $\pi^{\text{se}}$, $\pi^{\text{ru}}$, and $\pi^{\text{de}}$ determine $\{y_{r,i,v}\}$, $\{D^z_{r,i}, \mathbf{f}^z_{r,i}, \mathcal{P}^z_{r,i}\}_{z=1}^{Z_{r,i}}$, and $\{\mathcal A_w\}$, respectively.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
Let $\mathcal U_w$ be the set of requests completed in window $w$ and $\mathcal U_{1:W}=\bigcup_{w=1}^{W}\mathcal U_w$. The normalized latency and energy cost of request $r$ is:
\begin{equation}
J_r=\alpha\frac{T_r^{\text{e2e}}}{T_0}
+\beta\frac{E_r^{\text{tot}}}{E_0},
\label{eq:request_cost_elara}
\end{equation}
where $T_0$ and $E_0$ are reference latency and energy scales used to
nondimensionalize the two cost components, and $\alpha,\beta\geq0$ with $\alpha+\beta=1$. ELARA minimizes the mean cost over all completed requests:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:elara_prob_form}
\mathscr{P}: \quad \underset{\pi^{\text{se}},\pi^{\text{ru}},\pi^{\text{de}}}{\operatorname{minimize}} \quad &\frac{1}{|\mathcal U_{1:W}|} \sum_{r\in\mathcal U_{1:W}}J_r \\
\text{subject to}\quad
&\eqref{eq:serving_unique_elara},
\eqref{eq:service_routing_policy},
&\tag{C1}\\
&\eqref{eq:link_load_model},
\eqref{eq:parallel_phase_delay_model},
&\tag{C2}\\
&\eqref{eq:memory_elara},
\eqref{eq:replica_count_elara},
\eqref{eq:deployment_transition},
&\tag{C3}
\end{align}
\begin{comment}
Constraint C1 enforces valid serving-satellite selection with per-segment routing decisions, C2 ensures feasible cross-slot multi-hop delivery, and C3 preserves replica-count and storage constraints after microservice redeployment.
\end{comment}
Constraint C1 enforces valid serving satellite selection and routing decisions for each segment. Constraint C2 ensures feasible cross-slot multi-hop delivery. Constraint C3 preserves the limits on replica count and storage after microservice redeployment.

%\subsection{Hierarchical Decomposition by Decision Cadence}
%Solving $\mathscr P$ monolithically is impractical: $\pi^{\mathrm{sel}}$ and $\pi^{\mathrm{ru}}$ must respond to individual requests and topology updates, whereas $\pi^{\mathrm{dep}}$ affects many subsequent requests. ELARA therefore separates the policy by decision cadence while retaining the common cost in \eqref{eq:request_cost_elara}. With $\mathbf X_w$ fixed, the request execution controller selects a serving satellite and route for each service stage. At the end of window $w$, the replica adaptation controller uses the outcomes in $\mathcal U_w$ to update $\mathbf X_{w+1}$. The stage rewards and deployment feedback in Sec.~\ref{sec:algo} are both derived from $J_r$, keeping the controllers aligned with \eqref{eq:elara_prob_form}.






\section{The Proposed ELARA Framework}
\label{sec:algo}
In this section, we present ELARA, a microservice orchestration framework that coordinates service routing and  service replica deployment in SCPNs.
\begin{comment}
For each request, ELARA leverages a PPO agent to select a serving satellite for every execution stage and delivers the intermediate data over time-varying ISLs through a data routing module. Furthermore, a multi-armed bandit-based replica adapter is introduced to update the service deployment periodically according to the trajectories of completed requests in a adaption window. The workflow is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:elara}.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{For each request, ELARA uses a GNN-enhanced PPO agent to select a serving satellite at every execution stage.} A routing module then delivers the intermediate data over ISLs that vary with time. ELARA also uses a contextual bandit to update service deployment periodically from the trajectories of requests completed during an adaptation window. The workflow is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:elara}.




\subsection{Serving Satellite Selection}

% ELARA formulates serving satellite selection as an Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (POMDP), where one episode executes one request from its ingress satellite to its egress satellite.
ELARA formulates serving satellite selection as a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP), where one episode executes one request from its ingress satellite to its egress satellite. A decision is made when the input of service $m_i$ is available at $u_{r,i}$ at time $\tau_{r,i}$.

\paragraph{State Space}
Let $\mathcal V'_r$ be the subset of $\mathcal V$ that contains the ingress satellite, the egress satellite, and every satellite hosting a replica of a service requested by $r$.
ELARA constructs a rooted connector from the ingress satellite to all nodes in $\mathcal V'_r$ by taking the union of their shortest paths from the root. Satellites on these paths that do not belong to $\mathcal V'_r$ are relay nodes. The graph induced by the terminal and relay nodes is $\mathcal G_r(t)=\big(\mathcal V_r,\mathcal E_r(t)\big)$.
This construction guarantees that all replicas relevant to the request are connected when the full constellation graph is connected. At each slot boundary, ELARA verifies the induced graph and reconstructs the routing connector only upon a connectivity violation.
Furthermore, ELARA stores the current adjacency and the next $H$ adjacency snapshots using sparse edge indices, where the future sequence is denoted by $\{\mathcal E_r(t+k)\}_{k=1}^{H}$ and $H$ is a fixed prediction horizon.
The future snapshots contain only the connectivity determined by orbital motion.
Based on this, the state at stage $i$ of $r$ at $t$ is denoted as:
\begin{equation}
s_{r,i}=\left(\mathcal G_r^a(t),\{\mathcal E_r(t+k)\}_{k=1}^{H},
\mathbf c_{r,i},\mathbf m_r,\mathbf b_r,\mathbf y_{r,1:i-1}\right),
\label{eq:new_ppo_state}
\end{equation}
where $t=s(\tau_{r,i})$, $\mathcal G_r^a(t)$ denotes the current attributed graph, $\mathbf c_{r,i}$ records the request context, and $\mathbf m_r$, $\mathbf b_r$, and $\mathbf y_{r,1:i-1}$ record the requested service sequence, data volumes, and previous serving-satellite selection decisions, respectively.
% where $t=s(\tau_{r,i})$ and $\mathcal G_r^a(t)$ is the attributed graph containing current node and edge measurements. The request vector $\mathbf c_{r,i}$ comprises the arrival time, current time, remaining fraction of the current slot, stage progress, current data volume, accumulated latency, and accumulated energy. The vectors $\mathbf m_r$, $\mathbf b_r$, and $\mathbf y_{r,1:i-1}$ contain the requested services, data volumes, and previous serving satellite selections.

\begin{figure}[!t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figs/elara.pdf}
	\vspace{-0.6cm}
	\caption{The overview of ELARA.}
	\label{fig:elara}
	\vspace{-0.5cm}
\end{figure}

For every satellite $v\in\mathcal V_r$, the node vector $\mathbf{h}_v$ records its orbital plane and position, computing capacity, computing efficiency, computing queue, computing power, node role, replica status, and whether it was selected earlier in the request. Each edge vector $\mathbf{q}_{e}$ records the effective rate, transmission efficiency, transmission queue, transmission power, and distance.
ELARA then applies sparse graph attention to the current attributed graph $\mathcal{G}^a_r$. 
%Let a directed edge $(u,v)\in\mathcal E_r(t)$ denote a communication link along which node $u$ sends a message to node $v$ during graph representation learning. 
% For a directed edge $(u,v)$, an edge encoder produces an attention bias and an edge message from its current attributes. One graph layer computes:
For a directed edge $(u,v)\in \mathcal E_r(t)$, ELARA maps the attribute vector $\mathbf q_{uv}$ to the scalar attention bias $b_e(\mathbf q_{uv})$ and the edge message $\mathbf W_e\mathbf q_{uv}$. This edge describes message passing from $u$ to $v$ during graph representation learning. Let $\operatorname{LN}(\cdot)$ denote layer normalization. One graph layer computes:
\begin{align}
e_{uv}^{(l)}&=\frac{(\mathbf W_q\mathbf h_v^{(l)})^{\mathsf T}
(\mathbf W_k\mathbf h_u^{(l)})}{\sqrt{d_h}}+b_e(\mathbf q_{uv}),\nonumber\\
\mathbf m_v^{(l)}&=\sum_{u\in\mathcal N_v}\omega_{uv}
(\mathbf W_z\mathbf h_u^{(l)}+\mathbf W_e\mathbf q_{uv}),\nonumber\\
\mathbf h_v^{(l+1)}&=\operatorname{LN}\!\left(
\mathbf h_v^{(l)}+\sigma(\mathbf W_o\mathbf m_v^{(l)})\right),
\label{eq:sparse_graph_attention}
\end{align}
\begin{comment}
where $\omega_{uv}$ is the softmax of $e_{uv}^{(l)}$ over incoming neighbors.
\end{comment}
where $l$ is the graph layer index; $\mathbf h_v^{(l)}$ and $\mathbf h_u^{(l)}$ are the hidden representations of nodes $v$ and $u$, respectively; $d_h$ is the hidden dimension; and $\mathbf W_q$, $\mathbf W_k$, $\mathbf W_z$, $\mathbf W_e$, and $\mathbf W_o$ are trainable projections for the query, key, node message, edge message, and output. The scalar $e_{uv}^{(l)}$ is the attention score, $\mathcal N_v=\{u:(u,v)\in\mathcal E_r(t)\}$ is the set of incoming neighbors of $v$, and $\omega_{uv}=\operatorname{softmax}_{u\in\mathcal N_v}(e_{uv}^{(l)})$ is the corresponding normalized attention weight. The vector $\mathbf m_v^{(l)}$ is the message aggregated at $v$ using these attention weights, $\sigma(\cdot)$ is a nonlinear activation function, and $\mathbf h_v^{(l+1)}$ is the updated representation of $v$.

\begin{comment}
To capture the future information of network connectivity, ELARA encodes the $H$ future adjacency snapshots, $\{\mathcal E_r(t+k)\}_{k=1}^{H}$, using a distinct offset embedding for each $k$. For each satellite node $v\in\mathcal V_r$, temporal attention fuses this sequence with its current representation to obtain the spatio-temporal node embedding $\mathbf z_v$.
\end{comment}
To capture future network connectivity, ELARA encodes the $H$ future adjacency snapshots, $\{\mathcal E_r(t+k)\}_{k=1}^{H}$, using a distinct offset embedding for each $k$. For every satellite node $v\in\mathcal V_r$, temporal attention combines this sequence with its current representation to obtain the node embedding $\mathbf z_v$, which represents the communication and computing state of $v$ together with its predicted connectivity.





\begin{comment}
To evaluate candidate satellites of the request $r$, ELARA embeds the requested service sequence and combines it with $\mathbf c_{r,i}$ to form the request embedding $\mathbf z_{r,i}$. Since the selection also depends on network-wide communication and computing conditions, ELARA uses $\mathbf z_{r,i}$ together with the current-node and egress-node embeddings to pool the node representations most relevant to the current decision:
\end{comment}
To support serving satellite selection at stage $i$ of request $r$, ELARA embeds the requested service sequence and combines it with $\mathbf c_{r,i}$ to form the request embedding $\mathbf z_{r,i}$. Since this decision also depends on communication and computing conditions across the network, ELARA uses $\mathbf z_{r,i}$ together with the embeddings of the current satellite and egress satellite to pool information from all nodes in $\mathcal V_r$, assigning greater weight to the nodes most relevant to the current decision:
\begin{align}
\mathbf q_g&=\mathbf W_p[\mathbf z_{r,i},\mathbf z_{u_{r,i}},\mathbf z_{v_d}],\nonumber\\
a_v^g&=(\mathbf q_g)^{\mathsf T}\mathbf W_k^g\mathbf z_v/\sqrt{d_h},\nonumber\\
\eta_v&=\operatorname{softmax}_{v}(a_v^g),\qquad
\mathbf h_g=\sum_{v\in\mathcal V_r}\eta_v\mathbf W_g\mathbf z_v,
\label{eq:global_graph_embedding}
\end{align}
where nodes $u_{r,i}$ and $v_d$ are the satellite currently holding the input of $m_i$ and the request's egress satellite, respectively, while $\mathbf z_{u_{r,i}}$ and $\mathbf z_{v_d}$ are their node embeddings. 
%The brackets $[\cdot]$ denote vector concatenation. 
\begin{comment}
The brackets $[\cdot]$ denote vector concatenation. The matrices $\mathbf W_p$, $\mathbf W_k^g$, and $\mathbf W_g$ are trainable projections for the pooling query, node keys, and node values, respectively. The vector $\mathbf q_g$ is the request-conditioned graph-pooling query, $a_v^g$ is the importance score of node $v$, $\eta_v=\exp(a_v^g)/\sum_{w\in\mathcal V_r}\exp(a_w^g)$ is its attention weight over the request-graph node set $\mathcal V_r$, and $\mathbf h_g$ is the resulting global graph embedding.
\end{comment}
The matrices $\mathbf W_p$, $\mathbf W_k^g$, and $\mathbf W_g$ are trainable projections for the pooling query, node keys, and node values, respectively. The vector $\mathbf q_g$ is the graph pooling query conditioned on the request, $a_v^g$ is the importance score of node $v$, $\eta_v=\exp(a_v^g)/\sum_{w\in\mathcal V_r}\exp(a_w^g)$ is its attention weight over the nodes in $\mathcal V_r$, and $\mathbf h_g$ is the resulting global graph embedding.

%\textcolor{blue}{For a request graph with $|\mathcal V_r|$ nodes and $|\mathcal E_r|$ edges, ELARA stores only the node features and sparse edge indices, requiring $\mathcal O(|\mathcal V_r|+|\mathcal E_r|)$ memory footprint. Each graph attention layer costs $\mathcal O(|\mathcal V_r|d_h^2+|\mathcal E_r|d_h)$, and request conditioned pooling costs $\mathcal O(|\mathcal V_r|d_h)$. 
%Neither operation constructs a dense $|\mathcal V_r|\times|\mathcal V_r|$ adjacency matrix or performs pairwise pooling over all nodes.}

\begin{comment}
Overall, the policy represents the state $s_{r,i}$ using the node embeddings $\{\mathbf z_v\}_{v\in\mathcal V_r}$, the request embedding $\mathbf z_{r,i}$, and the global graph embedding $\mathbf h_g$, while retaining $\mathbf z_{u_{r,i}}$ and $\mathbf z_{v_d}$ to identify the current and egress satellites. These representations capture the current communication and computing conditions, the predicted topology evolution, and the service-chain context of the ongoing request. Candidate-specific routing and queue measurements are appended after the legal action set is constructed, as described next.
\end{comment}
The policy represents state $s_{r,i}$ through the node embeddings $\{\mathbf z_v\}_{v\in\mathcal V_r}$, request embedding $\mathbf z_{r,i}$, and global graph embedding $\mathbf h_g$. The embeddings $\mathbf z_{u_{r,i}}$ and $\mathbf z_{v_d}$ identify the current satellite and egress satellite. Together, these quantities describe the current network and request contexts, including communication and computing conditions, predicted topology, requested service sequence, and execution progress.
%After the legal actions are determined, the path and queue measurements of each feasible satellite are added to its scoring input.




\paragraph{Action Space}
Based on the above representations, the legal actions are the satellites that currently host $m_i$:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal A(s_{r,i})=\{v\in\mathcal V_{m_i}(t)\cap\mathcal V_r:
d^{\text{hop}}(u_{r,i},v)<\infty\},
\end{equation}
\begin{comment}
where $m_i$ is the microservice required at stage $i$; $\mathcal V_{m_i}(t)$ is the set of satellites hosting an active replica of $m_i$ in slot $t$. The function $d^{\text{hop}}(u_{r,i},v)$ calculates the number of ISL hops on a current shortest path from $u_{r,i}$ to $v$, with $\infty$ indicating that $v$ is unreachable. Thus, each $v\in\mathcal A(s_{r,i})$ represents one feasible serving-satellite selection action.

For every candidate $v \in \mathcal A(s_{r,i})$, ELARA calculates the current hop distance $d_v^{\text{hop}}$, the effective bottleneck rate $R_v^{\text{btl}}$ on its shortest path, and its computing queue $Q_v^{\mathrm{cp}}$. Specifically, $d_v^{\text{hop}}=d^{\text{hop}}(u_{r,i},v)$, $R_v^{\text{btl}}$ is the minimum current effective rate among the ISLs on that shortest path, and $Q_v^{\text{cp}}$ is the current unfinished computing workload queued at $v$. A shared scorer evaluates every candidate with the same parameters:
\end{comment}
where $\mathcal V_{m_i}(t)$ is the subset of satellites hosting an active replica of microservice $m_i$, which is required at stage $i$. The function $d^{\text{hop}}(u_{r,i},v)$ gives the number of hops on the current shortest path from $u_{r,i}$ to $v$ and is set to $\infty$ when no path exists. For each $v\in\mathcal A(s_{r,i})$, $d_v^{\text{hop}}=d^{\text{hop}}(u_{r,i},v)$, $R_v^{\text{btl}}$ is the minimum effective ISL rate along this path, and $Q_v^{\text{cp}}$ is the current computing queue at $v$. A shared function scores every candidate as follows:
\begin{equation}
g_v=f_\theta\!\left(\mathbf z_v,\mathbf z_{u_{r,i}},\mathbf z_{v_d},
\mathbf z_{r,i},\mathbf h_g,d_v^{\text{hop}},R_v^{\text{btl}},Q_v^{\mathrm{cp}}\right).
\label{eq:new_shared_scorer}
\end{equation}
\begin{comment}
where $f_\theta(\cdot)$ is the candidate scoring network parameterized by $\theta$, and $g_v$ is its scalar logit for candidate $v$. A masked softmax normalizes $\{\exp(g_v):v\in\mathcal A(s_{r,i})\}$ and assigns zero probability to all illegal satellites, yielding the policy probability $\pi_\theta(v\mid s_{r,i})$ of selecting $v$ in state $s_{r,i}$. This shared construction supports a different replica count for every service without changing the output dimension of the network.
\end{comment}
where $f_\theta(\cdot)$ is a scoring function with parameters $\theta$, and $g_v$ is the score assigned to $v$. Since $f_\theta(\cdot)$ evaluates each feasible satellite separately, it can handle services with different numbers of replicas. 
A masked softmax over the resulting scores gives the selection probability $\pi_\theta(v\mid s_{r,i})$.

\paragraph{Reward Function}
For an action that selects $v_{r,i}$, the incremental latency and energy are formulated as:
\begin{align}
T_{r,i}^{\text{step}}&=T_{r,i}^{\text{ru}}+T_{r,i}^{\text{cp}}
+\mathbb I_{i=L}T_{r,L+1}^{\text{ru}},\nonumber\\
E_{r,i}^{\text{step}}&=E_{r,i}^{\text{ru}}+E_{r,i}^{\text{cp}}
+\mathbb I_{i=L}E_{r,L+1}^{\text{ru}}.
\label{eq:new_ppo_step_cost}
\end{align}
The indicator assigns the latency and energy of final output delivery only to the last action.
The reward is calculated by:
\begin{equation}
r_{r,i}=-\alpha\frac{T_{r,i}^{\text{step}}}{T_0}
-\beta\frac{E_{r,i}^{\text{step}}}{E_0},
\label{eq:new_ppo_reward}
\end{equation}
%When $\gamma=1$, $\sum_i r_{r,i}=-J_r$. 
where $\alpha + \beta =1$.
A routing failure terminates the episode and returns a fixed negative penalty. 
%No terminal latency or energy term is added because the complete request cost has already been assigned to its stages.

\paragraph{Transition and PPO Update}
After $v_{r,i}$ is selected, the routing module delivers $B_{r,i}$ from $u_{r,i}$ to $v_{r,i}$, and the selected satellite $v_{r,i}$ executes $m_i$. The next state begins at $u_{r,i+1}=v_{r,i}$ when this computation finishes. The episode terminates after the final service completes and its output is delivered to $v_d$.

During training, the critic $V_\phi(s_{r,i})$ estimates the expected return from state $s_{r,i}$. Using generalized advantage estimation (GAE) to obtain $\widehat A_j$, ELARA trains the agent by minimizing:
\begin{align}
\mathcal L(\theta,\phi)=&-\mathbb E_j\!\left[\min(\rho_j\widehat A_j,
\bar\rho_j\widehat A_j)\right]
+c_v\mathbb E_j\!\left[(V_\phi(s_j)-\widehat V_j)^2\right]\nonumber\\
&-\eta_H\mathbb E_j[\mathcal H(\pi_\theta(\cdot\mid s_j))],
\label{eq:new_ppo_loss}
\end{align}
\begin{comment}
where $j$ is the index of a transition sampled from the PPO rollout buffer, $\rho_j=\pi_{\theta}(a_j|s_j)/\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(a_j|s_j)$ is the original probability ratio, and $\bar{\rho}_j=\text{clip}(\rho_j,1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)$ is the clipped ratio. $\hat{V}_j$ is the value target and $\mathcal{H}_j=\mathcal{H}(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|s_j))$ is the entropy term that encourages policy exploration.
\end{comment}
where $j$ indexes a transition $(s_j,a_j)$ sampled from the PPO rollout buffer, $\theta$ and $\phi$ are the actor and critic parameters, and $\mathbb E_j[\cdot]$ denotes the expectation over sampled transitions. The probability ratio is $\rho_j=\pi_\theta(a_j\mid s_j)/\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(a_j\mid s_j)$, where $\theta_{\mathrm{old}}$ denotes the actor parameters used to collect the rollout. Its clipped value is $\bar\rho_j=\operatorname{clip}(\rho_j,1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)$, with $\epsilon$ controlling the clipping range. The value target is $\widehat V_j$, and $\mathcal H(\pi_\theta(\cdot\mid s_j))$ is the policy entropy. The coefficients $c_v$ and $\eta_H$ weight the value loss and entropy term, respectively.


\subsection{Data Routing across Time Slots}

\begin{comment}
For a selected source $u$, destination $v$, and data volume $B$, ELARA reserves complete paths on the current topology. It attempts delivery over at most $W_{\text{ru}}$ slots and uses at most $K_{\text{max}}$ path augmentations in each slot.

At time $\tau$ in slot $t$, let $\Delta_t$ be the time remaining before the next topology update. The usable data capacity of edge $e$ is:
\begin{equation}
C_e^{\text{res}}(t,\tau)=
\frac{R_e(t)}{8000}
\left[\Delta_t-\nu^{\text{qe}}_e(t)-\nu^{\text{pr}}_e(t)-\nu^{\text{sw}}\right]^+,
\label{eq:new_residual_capacity}
\end{equation}
where $[x]^+=\max\{x,0\}$, $T_e^{\text{cm}}(D_e,t)$ is calculated by \eqref{eq:link_delay_elara}, and $\nu^{\text{sw}}$ is the switching overhead. The explanation of the factor $8000$ was not present in the original text.
where $[x]^+=\max\{x,0\}$. Since $R_e(t)$ is measured in megabits per second (Mbps) and the terms in brackets are measured in seconds, division by $8000$ converts megabits to gigabytes (GB), using $1~\mathrm{GB}=8000~\mathrm{Mb}$. $\nu^{\text{sw}}$ is the switching overhead.
%The default router does not add a future disconnection penalty. 
The edge cost is formulated as:
\begin{align}
c_e(t,\tau)=
&\frac{\alpha}{T_0}\left(T_e^{\text{cm}}(D_e, t)+\nu^{\text{sw}}\right)+\frac{\beta P_e^{\text{tx}}8000}{E_0 R_e(t)},
\label{eq:new_routing_edge_cost}
\end{align}
which measures the latency and energy of transmitting one GB over edge $e$ using measurements from the current slot.

ELARA repeatedly finds a minimum cost path in the residual graph. For a path $p$, it admits the largest amount that satisfies every edge capacity and reaches $v$ before the slot boundary. Distinct path flows proceed in parallel. Hops on one path are serialized because a satellite receives the admitted block before forwarding it. If several paths share an ISL, their aggregate traffic is serialized on that ISL. ELARA recalculates every path completion time with this aggregate load before committing the phase. The phase duration is:
ELARA repeatedly finds a path with the minimum cost in the residual graph. For each path $p$, it admits the largest data volume that satisfies all edge capacities and can reach $v$ before the slot boundary. Data blocks assigned to different paths are transmitted in parallel. Along one path, each satellite receives the complete block before forwarding it, while reservations that share an ISL are transmitted sequentially. Before accepting the phase, ELARA recomputes every path completion time using the total reserved load on each shared ISL. The phase duration is:
\begin{equation}
\Delta_t^{\text{ru}}=\max_{p\in\mathcal P_t}
\left\{\sum_{e\in p}\left(
\frac{8000F_e(t)}{R_e(t)}+\frac{D_e}{c}+Q_e(\tau)+\nu^{\text{sw}}
\right)\right\},
\label{eq:new_phase_duration}
\end{equation}
where $F_e(t)$ is the aggregate admitted data on edge $e$. The phase is committed only when $\Delta_t^{\text{ru}}\leq\Delta_t$.
After a committed phase, the delivered blocks are available at $v$. Any undelivered part remains at $u$. At the next slot boundary, ELARA obtains the new topology and computes new complete paths from $u$ to $v$, ensuring no partial block is left at a relay satellite when the topology changes.

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\color{blue}
\SetAlFnt{\small}
\SetAlgoLined
\caption{Routing with Complete Path Reservation across Slots}
\label{algo:new_cross_slot_routing}
\SetKwInOut{Input}{Input}
\SetKwInOut{Output}{Output}
\Input{Source $u$, destination $v$, volume $B$, and start time $\tau$}
\Output{Delivery time, energy, and reservations}
\For{$h=1$ \KwTo $W_{\text{ru}}$}{
	Construct the residual graph for the current slot\;
	\For{$k=1$ \KwTo $K_{\max}$}{
		Find a minimum cost path from $u$ to $v$\;
		\If{no feasible path exists}{break\;}
		Compute the largest admissible volume on the path\;
		Update temporary edge capacities and aggregate edge loads\;
	}
	Recalculate all path completion times using aggregate edge loads\;
	\If{the phase fits in the current slot}{commit its reservations and update $B$\;}
	\If{$B=0$}{\Return{delivery time, energy, and reservations}\;}
	Keep the residual data at $u$ and advance to the next slot\;
}
\Return{no feasible delivery within the routing horizon}\;
\end{algorithm}
\end{comment}

\begingroup
\color{blue}
%The ISL topology may change before a service input reaches its destination. ELARA therefore reserves only complete paths whose assigned data can reach destination $v$ within the current slot. This rule prevents data from remaining at a relay satellite when the topology is updated. 
\begin{comment}
Given source $u$, destination $v$, data volume $B$, and start time $\tau$, the data routing module searches at most $H$ slots and admits at most $|\mathcal P|$ paths per slot.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{Given source $u$, destination $v$, data volume $B$, and start time $\tau$, ELARA treats routing in each slot as a minimum cost maximum flow problem subject to a slot completion constraint. It maximizes the volume delivered before the topology update while minimizing its weighted latency and energy cost. The search spans at most $H$ slots and admits at most $|\mathcal P|$ paths per slot.}
In slot $t$, let $\Delta_t$ denote the time remaining until the next topology update. ELARA first calculates the amount of data that each edge $e$ can still carry:
\begin{equation}
C_e^{\text{res}}(t,\tau)=
\frac{R_e(t)}{8000}
\left[\Delta_t-\nu^{\text{qe}}_e(t)-\nu^{\text{pr}}_e(t)-\nu^{\text{sw}}\right]^+,
\label{eq:new_residual_capacity}
\end{equation}
where $[x]^+=\max\{x,0\}$ and $\nu^{\text{sw}}$ is the switching overhead. Since $R_e(t)$ is measured in Mbps and data volume is measured in GB, the factor $8000$ follows from $1~\mathrm{GB}=8000~\mathrm{Mb}$. 
%Edges with zero residual capacity are removed. 
The cost of transmitting one GB on each remaining edge is:
\begin{align}
c_e(t,\tau)=
&\alpha \frac{\left( T^{\text{cm}}_e(t)+\nu^{\text{sw}}\right) }{T_0}
+\beta\frac{ P_e^{\text{tx}}8000}{E_0R_e(t)}.
\label{eq:new_routing_edge_cost}
\end{align}
%The two terms represent normalized communication time and transmission energy, so path selection follows the request objective.

\begin{comment}
No corresponding original text. The following sentence was added to explain how the edge capacities and costs define the minimum cost maximum flow problem.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{Together, $C_e^{\text{res}}(t,\tau)$ and $c_e(t,\tau)$ define the capacity and unit cost of edge $e$ in the residual graph.}

\begin{comment}
ELARA repeatedly finds the path with the smallest total edge cost and tentatively assigns the largest volume allowed by the residual data and all edges on that path. The search stops when the data is fully assigned, no feasible path remains, or $|\mathcal P|$ paths have been examined. Different paths transmit in parallel, while the hops on each path proceed in sequence. Volumes assigned to paths that share an ISL are added on that link.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{ELARA applies the successive shortest path method. Each iteration finds the least costly augmenting path, pushes its largest feasible data volume, and updates the residual capacities. The process stops when all data is assigned, no augmenting path remains, or $|\mathcal P|$ paths have been admitted. Different paths transmit in parallel, while the hops on each path proceed in sequence. Volumes on a shared ISL are added together.}
Before committing these reservations, ELARA verifies their completion times using the aggregate load $\mathbf{f}_e(t)$ with:
\begin{equation}
\Delta_t^{\text{ru}}=\max_{p\in\mathcal P_t}
\left\{\sum_{e\in p}\left(
\frac{8000\mathbf{f}_e(t)}{R_e(t)}+\nu_e^{\text{pr}}(t)
+\nu_e^{\text{qe}}(t)+\nu^{\text{sw}}
\right)\right\}.
\label{eq:new_phase_duration}
\end{equation}
\begin{comment}
A path assignment is accepted only if $\Delta_t^{\text{ru}}\leq\Delta_t$; otherwise, its volume is reduced or the path is discarded. After the accepted reservations are committed, ELARA removes the delivered volume from $B$ and records its delay and energy. Any residual data remains at $u$. At the next slot boundary, the module rebuilds the residual graph using the new topology and repeats the procedure. Routing succeeds when all data reaches $v$ and fails if data remains after $H$ slots.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{A reservation is committed only if $\Delta_t^{\text{ru}}\leq\Delta_t$; otherwise, its volume is reduced or its path is discarded. ELARA removes delivered data from $B$, records its delay and energy, and keeps any remainder at $u$. The residual graph is rebuilt after each topology update. Routing fails if data remains after $H$ slots.}
