\section{Performance Evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}

%This section evaluates ELARA under dynamic LEO constellation topologies and microservice workloads.
%We aim to answer the following questions: (i) whether ELARA reduces end-to-end latency and energy compared with representative service orchestration baselines; (ii) whether its major components are all necessary; (iii) how the service-routing module behaves under different chain lengths; and (iv) whether adaptive replica redeployment provides sustained benefits over time.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\begin{comment}
We implement ELARA and all baselines in a Python-based event-driven simulator.
The simulator replays time-varying LEO topology snapshots, maintains dynamic onboard computing queues and ISL queues, and records request-level and hop-level execution traces.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{We implement ELARA and all baselines in an event driven simulator written in Python.
The simulator replays the LEO topology snapshots in slot order. Target request arrivals follow a Poisson process. All target requests whose arrival times fall in the current slot are processed before the simulator advances to the next slot. Each target request is an independent episode. Its communication and computation reservations are discarded when the episode ends, including when multiple target requests arrive in the same slot. Consequently, the arrival intensity determines only the number and arrival times of target requests, and target requests never compete with one another for an ISL or an onboard processor.}
Unless otherwise specified, each reported result is averaged over four random seeds.
