\subsection{Baselines and Metrics}
We compare ELARA with the following baselines:
\begin{comment}
The original descriptions were:
\begin{verbatim}
\item \textbf{SECO}~\cite{seco_infocom2024}: a satellite edge computing baseline that performs queue-aware processing and routing decisions for each request.
\item \textbf{SC-NFV}~\cite{sc_nfv_tmc2024}: a service-chaining baseline adapted from NFV-enabled satellite edge networks to the considered microservice execution setting.
\item \textbf{SP-Routing}~\cite{service_pressure}: a service-pressure routing baseline, inspired by backpressure, that favors nodes and paths with smaller virtual backlog.
\end{verbatim}
\end{comment}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{SECO}~\cite{seco_infocom2024}: \textcolor{blue}{a satellite edge computing baseline that uses queue information for processing and routing decisions for each request.}
\item \textbf{SC-NFV}~\cite{sc_nfv_tmc2024}: \textcolor{blue}{a service chaining baseline for network function virtualization (NFV) in satellite edge networks, adapted to the considered microservice execution setting.}
\item \textbf{SP-Routing}~\cite{service_pressure}: \textcolor{blue}{a service pressure (SP) routing baseline, inspired by backpressure, that favors nodes and paths with smaller virtual backlogs.}
\end{itemize}

For the ablation study, we further consider the following ELARA variants:
\begin{comment}
The original descriptions were:
\begin{verbatim}
\item \textbf{ELARA-NB}: disables bandit-based replica redeployment.
\item \textbf{ELARA-NR}: replaces GNN-PPO serving satellite selection with nearest-replica selection.
\item \textbf{ELARA-SH}: replaces the min-cost max-flow routing module with shortest-hop routing.
\end{verbatim}
\end{comment}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{ELARA-NB}: \textcolor{blue}{disables replica redeployment by the bandit.}
\item \textbf{ELARA-NR}: \textcolor{blue}{replaces serving satellite selection by GNN and PPO with selection of the nearest replica.}
\item \textbf{ELARA-SH}: \textcolor{blue}{replaces routing based on minimum cost maximum flow with routing by the shortest hop count.}
\end{itemize}

\begin{comment}
We evaluate all methods using mean end-to-end latency, mean execution energy, success rate, deadline acceptance rate, mean communication delay, and mean time-slot crossings.
End-to-end latency includes both data routing and onboard computation, while execution energy includes communication and computation energy.
Time-slot crossings measure how often service-stage data transmission spans multiple time slots, thereby reflecting the ability of a method to handle dynamic ISL changes.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{We evaluate all methods using mean total latency, mean execution energy, success rate, deadline acceptance rate, mean communication delay, and mean number of slot crossings.
Total latency includes both data routing and onboard computation, while execution energy includes communication and computation energy.
The number of slot crossings measures how often data transmission for a service stage spans multiple slots, thereby reflecting the ability of a method to handle dynamic ISL changes.}

