\subsection{Performance Evaluation}

\paragraph{Overall Performance.}
\begin{comment}
Fig.~\ref{fig:elara_train} reports the training dynamics of the proposed GNN-PPO serving-node selector agent.
The reward curve gradually stabilizes and the PPO loss converges after training, indicating that the GNN-PPO selector learns a stable policy under dynamic topology traces.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{Fig.~\ref{fig:elara_train} reports the training dynamics of the proposed serving satellite selector based on GNN and PPO.
The reward curve gradually stabilizes and the PPO loss converges, indicating that the selector learns a stable policy from dynamic topology traces.}

Fig.~\ref{fig:elara_comparison} compares ELARA with the representative baselines.
\begin{comment}
ELARA achieves the lowest average end-to-end latency of 35.37 s, reducing latency by 4.4\%, 17.2\%, and 28.3\% compared with SECO, SC-NFV, and SP-Routing, respectively.
The improvement is especially significant over the SP-Routing and SC-NFV baselines, since ELARA jointly accounts for serving-satellite selection, cross-slot ISL routing, and onboard computation states.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{ELARA achieves the lowest average total latency of 35.37 s, reducing latency by 4.4\%, 17.2\%, and 28.3\% compared with SECO, SC-NFV, and SP-Routing, respectively.
The improvement is especially significant over SP-Routing and SC-NFV because ELARA jointly accounts for serving satellite selection, ISL routing across slots, and onboard computation states.}
For mean execution energy, ELARA incurs 1.49 kJ, which is comparable to SECO and 16.6\% and 20.7\% lower than SC-NFV and SP-Routing, respectively.
\begin{comment}
These results show that optimizing routing pressure or service-chain feasibility alone is insufficient, because serving-satellite selection directly affects both communication and computation costs.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{These results show that optimizing routing pressure or service chain feasibility alone is insufficient because serving satellite selection directly affects both communication and computation costs.}

Fig.~\ref{fig:elara_ablation} studies the contribution of each ELARA component.
Removing the adaptive redeployment module increases the average latency from 35.37 s to 37.50 s, proving the importance of adapting the replica placement as request patterns and topology states evolve.
\begin{comment}
Replacing the GNN-PPO node selector with nearest-replica selection causes the largest degradation. ELARA-NR increases mean latency by 54.9\% and mean execution energy by 72.6\%, because it ignores onboard computation load, future routing risk, and ISL states, indicating that a satellite hosting a geographically nearby replica can be overloaded or poorly connected.
Replacing min-cost max-flow routing with shortest-hop routing also degrades performance.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{Replacing the node selector based on GNN and PPO with selection of the nearest replica causes the largest degradation. ELARA-NR increases mean latency by 54.9\% and mean execution energy by 72.6\% because it ignores onboard computation load, future routing risk, and ISL states. A satellite hosting a geographically nearby replica can therefore be overloaded or poorly connected.
Replacing routing based on minimum cost maximum flow with routing by shortest hop count also degrades performance.}
ELARA-SH increases average latency by 34.7\% and reduces the deadline acceptance rate from 99.98\% to 98.10\%.
\begin{comment}
This result demonstrates that hop count alone is a weak routing objective in dynamic LEO networks, where link availability, effective rate, queuing delay, and residual cross-slot delivery jointly determine routing quality.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{This result demonstrates that hop count alone is a weak routing objective in dynamic LEO networks, where link availability, effective rate, queuing delay, and residual delivery across slots jointly determine routing quality.}

