\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{elara_exp/service_routing_analysis.pdf}
\vspace{-0.6cm}
\caption{\textcolor{blue}{Service routing analysis against ELARA variants.}}
\label{fig:service_routing_analysis}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{elara_exp/redeployment_adaptivity.pdf}
\vspace{-0.6cm}
\caption{Adaptivity of replica redeployment.}
\label{fig:redeployment_adaptivity}
%	\vspace{-0.7cm}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Service Routing Analysis}
\begin{comment}
Fig.~\ref{fig:service_routing_analysis_comparison} further examines request-level and hop-level behavior under different chain lengths.
Across the analyzed traces, ELARA reduces end-to-end latency by about 15--24\% compared with SECO, SC-NFV, and SP-Routing.
Its mean execution energy is also consistently lower, with reductions of about 14--25\%.
The gain persists for longer service chains: for chain length 15, ELARA lowers the end-to-end latency by 10.6\% over SECO, 19.2\% over SC-NFV, and 29.0\% over SP-Routing.
These results show that ELARA's serving-node selection is not only beneficial for isolated service stages, but also accumulates gains over multi-stage microservice execution.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{Fig.~\ref{fig:service_routing_analysis_comparison} further examines the behavior of requests and individual hops under different chain lengths.
Across the analyzed traces, ELARA reduces total latency by about 15\% to 24\% compared with SECO, SC-NFV, and SP-Routing.
Its mean execution energy is also consistently lower, with reductions of about 14\% to 25\%.
The gain persists for longer service chains. For a chain length of 15, ELARA lowers total latency by 10.6\% over SECO, 19.2\% over SC-NFV, and 29.0\% over SP-Routing.
These results show that ELARA's serving satellite selection benefits individual service stages and accumulates gains over execution with multiple stages.}

Fig.~\ref{fig:service_routing_analysis} compares ELARA with its variants using common request traces.
ELARA reduces the average communication delay per hop by roughly 24\% over ELARA-NB and 43\% over ELARA-SH.
\begin{comment}
The shortest-hop variant suffers from larger communication delay because it may choose fewer-hop paths with insufficient effective rate or higher queuing delay.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{The variant using the shortest hop count suffers from longer communication delay because it may choose paths with fewer hops but an insufficient effective rate or a longer queuing delay.}
In contrast, ELARA evaluates residual slot capacity and future link risk, allowing it to split traffic over multiple feasible paths when this lowers the overall delivery cost.
\begin{comment}
ELARA-NR has shorter communication delay because it selects nearby replicas, but its much larger computation delay dominates the end-to-end latency.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{ELARA-NR has a shorter communication delay because it selects nearby replicas, but its much longer computation delay dominates the total latency.}
This confirms that proximity alone is misleading when onboard computation heterogeneity and queuing states dominate service execution.

\paragraph{Adaptivity of Replica Redeployment}
\begin{comment}
Fig.~\ref{fig:redeployment_adaptivity} evaluates the slow-timescale replica redeployment module.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{Fig.~\ref{fig:redeployment_adaptivity} evaluates the periodic replica redeployment module.}
Panel (a) compares ELARA with ELARA-NB over redeployment windows, each comprising 10 time slots.
ELARA achieves positive latency reduction in all 60 redeployment windows, with a mean reduction of 9.6\%, while also reducing mean execution energy by 4.3\%.
This confirms that monitoring recent service pressure, routing failures, and computation waiting time helps the system move replicas toward more suitable orbital regions.

Panel (b) shows the distribution of applied redeployment actions.
Among 944 redeployment actions, 896 actions are replica movements and 48 actions are replica additions; thus, 94.9\% of the actions reallocate existing service capacity.
This behavior is desirable in SCPNs because service image transfer consumes ISL bandwidth and onboard storage is limited.
\begin{comment}
The results demonstrate that replica redeployment complements online routing: fast decisions handle per-request execution under the current topology, while periodic redeployment reshapes the service layout according to accumulated workload and network feedback.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{The results demonstrate that replica redeployment complements online routing. Immediate decisions handle each request under the current topology, while periodic redeployment reshapes the service layout according to accumulated workload and network feedback.}



\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\begin{comment}
Recent advances in satellite edge computing and SCPNs have enabled data processing directly in LEO constellations with onboard computation and ISL connectivity~\cite{shi2025satellite,survey_on_nextg_comp_tech_SAGIN,xie2020stec}.
\end{comment}
Recent advances in satellite edge computing and SCPNs have enabled data processing directly in LEO constellations with onboard computation and ISL connectivity~\cite{shi2025satellite,shen2023survey,xie2020stec}.
\begin{comment}
No corresponding original sentence. This sentence was added to cite recent work on satellite learning and large artificial intelligence models.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{Recent studies have also extended onboard intelligence to federated fine tuning of foundation models, decentralized learning inspired by the brain, and collaborative inference and training of large artificial intelligence models across satellites~\cite{sat_fed_finetune_twc2025,brain_decentralized_tmc2025,sai_lam_icc2025,lam_training_globecom2025}.}
Zhang et al.~\cite{zhang2023sec_offloading} investigated energy efficient peer offloading among satellites, while Leyva-Mayorga et al.~\cite{leyva2023satellite} and Zhai et al.~\cite{seco_infocom2024} explored Earth observation with strict timing requirements and joint optimization of observation scheduling, routing, and computation node selection.
These studies primarily focus on task offloading, processing for a specific mission, or joint allocation of computation and communication resources, whereas microservice provisioning introduces execution dependencies among distributed replicas at a finer granularity.

Service routing and task scheduling in satellite networks have also been studied from multiple perspectives.
Cao et al.~\cite{cao_comp_routing} proposed routing that considers computing states for remote sensing tasks, Xia et al.~\cite{sc_nfv_tmc2024} investigated service chaining with network function virtualization, and He et al.~\cite{service_pressure} developed routing for microservice flow throughput. Other studies model dynamic topologies using graphs expanded over time or optimize routing over LEO constellations that vary over time~\cite{liu2023time_expanded_graph,routing_optim_in_multi_domain,cross_slot_optim_infocom2025}.
However, most of these works either focus on communication paths alone or consider computation node selection within static topology snapshots or predefined sequences, leaving the joint impact of ISL disruptions, heterogeneous computing capacities, and topology variation insufficiently addressed.

Microservice deployment and request execution have been extensively studied in terrestrial edge systems.
Peng et al.~\cite{optim_svc_dep_req_reouting_in_MEC} studied joint service deployment and request routing in mobile edge computing. In satellite environments, Gao et al.~\cite{optim4server_svc_place_in_STIECN} investigated joint server and service selection, and Yu et al.~\cite{yu_deploy_tmc2025} developed robust reinforcement learning for microservice deployment in SCPNs.
Nevertheless, existing methods are either designed for static terrestrial scenarios or induce prohibitively large decision spaces when satellite selection and service deployment are considered together. Coordinating request execution with replica deployment under dynamic computing conditions and changing ISLs therefore remains an open challenge.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
%In this paper, we proposed a DST-based solution to provide an energy-efficient service routing scheme for tasks from terrestrial clients. We systematically developed the SCPN models, including communication and computation, covering various aspects of the LEO satellite network. We also mathematically formulated the energy optimization problem for satellite-microservice routing and transformed it into finding a feasible DST solution within an augmented directed satellite-microservice colocated graph. To approximate the optimal solution, we designed a series of heuristic algorithms. Experimental results show that our algorithm significantly outperforms other benchmarks in energy efficiency, robustness, and adaptability across different network and microservice configurations in the context of SCPN.

\begin{comment}
This paper presented ELARA, an online microservice orchestration framework for dynamic SCPNs. Under one objective over the decision horizon, its request execution controller selects serving satellites and reserves ISL capacity along complete paths, while its periodic deployment controller adapts replicas using request outcomes collected over each window. Undelivered data remains at the source and is routed again after each topology update. For up to $K_{\mathrm m}$ services with the highest pressure, a shared contextual bandit retains the current deployment or relocates one replica to another orbital plane subject to available memory. Relocation updates the active replica set using images stored locally on each satellite. Simulations with dynamic LEO traces show that coordinating request execution, routing, and replica adaptation reduces latency and energy compared with representative baselines.
\end{comment}
\textcolor{blue}{This paper presented ELARA, an online microservice orchestration framework for dynamic SCPNs. ELARA jointly selects serving satellites, routes data across changing ISL topologies, and periodically adapts service replicas under a common latency and energy objective. Its routing method keeps undelivered data at the source when the topology changes, while a contextual bandit adjusts services with high pressure under satellite memory constraints. Simulations using dynamic LEO traces show that this coordinated design reduces latency and energy compared with representative baselines.}



